# The ten tasks of the P1 protocol's SECOND round, fixed before the mechanism it
# will measure was built.
#
# WHY A SECOND SET. Round 1 measured the first promise and it did not survive as
# written. The cause was measured rather than inferred — `mcp_asked` false in
# twenty of twenty instrumented cells: the agent never called the record. The
# product's answer to that is a surface that serves the record unasked and charges
# for it, and proving the answer worked needs tasks nobody has spent. Round 1's
# four held-out axis-A tasks are burned, and its two development tasks were
# iterated against, so both classes are contaminated for a new measurement. These
# ten are new, and they were frozen BEFORE any of that surface exists — which is
# the whole reason this file is dated before the code it will judge.
#
# Each hash covers one task directory whole — its starting repository, its ticket,
# its decision, its two references and its discriminant — over a DETERMINISTIC
# archive: entry order fixed, timestamps and ownership zeroed, Python bytecode
# excluded. The archive carries the task's id as its path prefix, so two tasks
# cannot swap contents without both hashes moving.
#
# The tasks themselves are withheld until they have been used. Until then this
# file is what a third party can check: not what a task says, but that it did not
# change between the freeze and the result.
#
# REPRODUCE — GNU tar 1.35 and coreutils sha256sum, run from the directory that
# holds the ten task directories:
#
#   for id in a7-partial-refund a8-installments a9-phone-format a10-stock-cost \
#             a11-tax-id a12-payment-allocation a13-holiday-shift a14-tie-break \
#             b3-duration-format b4-run-length; do
#     printf '%s  %s\n' "$(tar --sort=name --format=gnu \
#         --mtime='UTC 1970-01-01' --owner=0 --group=0 --numeric-owner \
#         --exclude='__pycache__' --exclude='*.pyc' \
#         -cf - "$id" | sha256sum | cut -d' ' -f1)" "$id"
#   done
#
# A hash nobody knows how to reproduce proves nothing, which is why the command is
# here and not in a report. A different tar implementation will produce a different
# stream: the version above is the one these hashes were taken with.
#
28e73a8d4ac39568c1c35a0905081d7dfc48001a396d013901e190869e2a592b  a7-partial-refund
